  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q1-7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p11-q1-7-stem",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "True or False?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "In $(a+b)^n$, the coefficient of $a^{n-k}b^k$ equals $\\binom{n}{k}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. The binomial theorem gives the term $\\binom{n}{k}a^{n-k}b^k$, so its coefficient is $\\binom{n}{k}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "In $(a+b)^n$, the coefficient of $a^kb^{n-k}$ is also $\\binom{n}{k}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. The coefficient is $\\binom{n}{n-k}=\\binom{n}{k}$ by symmetry.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "The powers of $x$ in the expansion of $(1+x^2)^n$ go up by 2 each term.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. The general term is $T_k=\\binom{n}{k}x^{2k}$. As $k$ increases by 1, the exponent increases by 2.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-7-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "The powers of $x$ in the expansion of $(x+x^2)^n$ go up by 3 each term.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. They go up by 1 each term.",
          "worked": "False.\n$$\\begin{align*}T_k&=\\binom{n}{k}x^{n-k}(x^2)^k\\\\T_k&=\\binom{n}{k}x^{n+k}\\end{align*}$$\nAs $k$ increases by 1, the exponent increases by 1.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-7-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "In $\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^n$, a non-zero term independent of $x$ occurs if $n$ is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True.\n$$\\begin{align*}T_k&=\\binom{n}{k}x^{n-k}(x^{-1})^k\\\\T_k&=\\binom{n}{k}x^{n-2k}\\end{align*}$$\nA constant term requires $n-2k=0$, giving $k=\\frac{n}{2}$. When $n$ is even, this is an integer and the constant term is $\\binom{n}{\\frac{n}{2}}$, which is non-zero.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-7-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "If $a$ and $b$ are integers, then $(a+b)^n+(a-b)^n$ is always an even number.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. In the two binomial expansions, terms with odd powers of $b$ cancel and terms with even powers of $b$ are doubled. Each remaining term is twice an integer, so the sum is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q1-7-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "If $a$ and $b$ are integers, then $(a+b)^n-(a-b)^n$ is always an odd number.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. It is always even.",
          "worked": "False. Subtracting the binomial expansions cancels terms with even powers of $b$ and doubles terms with odd powers of $b$. Each remaining term is twice an integer, so the difference is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
